h that $1 \\le j < i.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a1e480bf649c245cf1ae35df0f7604cca1f570f5a9bfd8d8f151b6c15b122cfe"},"seconds":54.01309776306152,"seed":506039236843282273,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given sum\n\\[\n\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\n\\]\nwe first need to understand the range of the indices \\(i\\) and \\(j\\). The condition \\(1 \\le j < i\\) means that \\(i\\) can take any positive integer value, and for each \\(i\\), \\(j\\) can take any integer value from 1 up to \\(i-1\\).\n\nLet's rewrite the sum by fixing \\(i\\) and summing over \\(j\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\), the inner sum over \\(j\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}}.\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\(\\frac{1}{2^i}\\) from this sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i+j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}.\n\\]\n\nThe inner sum is a geometric series:\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j} = \\frac{1/2 (1 - (1/2)^{i-1})}{1 - 1/2} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the inner sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute this back into the outer sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nWe can split this into two separate sums:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i}\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{1}{2^2} + \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^4} + \\cdots = \\frac{1/4}{1 - 1/2} = \\frac{1/4}{1/2} = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^5} + \\frac{1}{2^7} + \\cdots = \\frac{1/8}{1 - 1/4} = \\frac{1/8}{3/4} = \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the original sum is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
